From koenig@tat.physik.uni-tuebingen.de Thu Aug 14 02:21:18 1997
Message-ID: <9708140720.AA18206@batu.hip.atr.co.jp>
Date: Thu, 14 Aug 1997 11:29:14 +0200
From: Harald Koenig <koenig@tat.physik.uni-tuebingen.de>
Subject: Re: Internal modem in DEC PC Axp 150 (alias Jensen)
In-Reply-To: <43u3gtgm50.fsf@comnets.rwth-aachen.de>

On Aug 14, Martin Ostermann wrote:

> OTOH, do you use an S3 card? I heard that S3 configuration registers
> use the same addresses as internal modems, commonly refered to COM4, I
> believe. So maybe you need to reconfigure your modem, to use other 
> addresses. Hmm, hopefully it isn't a PnP modem, or is it?

it's not the same address.  

COM4 is [only] 0x02e8, but poor serial cards don't decode all 16 I/O address bits,
but only 10 bits (like for good old IBM PC) so this port gets replicated and
only then gives address collisions with every IBM 8514 compatible chip
(S3, mach8/32 etc.).  

S3 only uses 0xN2e8 with "N" being 4,8,9,A,B,E but it doesn't use 0x02e8.
               ^          ^                                         ^
               ^          ^                                         ^

Harald

